ntents\n\n- `data/historical/`: exact original historical CSV and README downloaded through NDP.\n- `data/2023-24/`: exact original target CSV and README.\n- `reproduce.py`: portable analysis and verification code; Python standard library only.\n- `reference_results/`: the three CSVs used in the conversation and seasonal-summary JSON.\n- `METHODS.md`: algorithms, definitions, field meanings and coverage caveats.\n- `PROVENANCE.json`: dataset/resource identities, original hashes, attribution and license notices.\n- `manifest.json`: SHA-256 hashes of bundled files, excluding the manifest itself.\n- `CLEAN_RERUN.md`: the author's extracted-copy test record.\n\nThe runner writes three CSVs plus `coverage.json`, `seasonal_summary.json`, `monthly_contributions.json` and `verification.json`. Verification requires byte-identical CSVs and equality of the parsed seasonal JSON. JSON whitespace and platform-specific line endings are not scientific differences.\n\n## Expected findings\n\n- October-April 2023-24: 635.5 inches; 17.3% above the 17-complete-winter mean; sixth of 18 including the target.\n- November-April: 606.5 inches; sixth of 25 including the target.\n- January-March: +151.1765 inches versus the matched historical monthly means; the other months offset this by 57.4118 inches.\n- February 2024: 154.5 inches; second of 25 Februaries when all complete historical Februaries are used.\n\nThe package reproduces numerical comparisons, not CLIO's interactive interface. Use the CSVs in a plotting tool of your choice.\n\n## Attribution and permitted use\n\nOriginal observations: Alta Ski Area Avalanche Office / Alta Ski Area. Research dataset creators: Michael Wasserstein and Jim Steenburgh, University of Utah; distributed by The Hive and discovered via NDP, publisher `marriott_library_hive`. The historical README specifies **CC BY-NC 4.0**; the target README specifies **CC BY NC** without a version. Preserve attribution and noncommercial restrictions; this package does not relicense the source data. Consult the bundled unmodified READMEs and DOI records for authoritative terms.\n\n- Historical DOI: https://doi.org/10.7278/S50d-nsy5-8bje\n- 2023-24 DOI: https://doi.org/10.7278/S5d-dx7x-d8ay\n\nDo not treat these 25 winters as an all-time Alta record or infer return periods from their ranks.\n","kind":"report","name":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-shareable-20261019\\alta-snowfall-17\\alta_reproducibility\\README.md"},{"annotation":"Explicit portable methods, data dictionary, ranking rules, rounding and coverage limits.","content":"# Methods, definitions and coverage\n\n## Inputs and station\n\nUse the original `Time (LST)` and `12 hr Snow (in.)` fields. Snowfall is newly accumulated snow in inches, not snow depth or liquid precipitation equivalent. The files also contain LPE, which is not used in these comparisons. The station is the Alta-Collins mid-mountain snow-study plot, approximately 40.57607 N, 111.63889 W, elevation 2945 m. Manual snowboard observations were taken by Alta Ski Patrol.\n\nThe historical file contains 9,940 observations for ending years 2000-2023; the target file has 426 observations for ending year 2024. These data snapshots are bundled as downloaded, not refreshed from the catalog during a rerun.\n\n## Time and completeness\n\nParse timestamps as `%Y-%m-%d %H` in local standard time. They name the START of a 12-hour observation period. Assign October-December to the next ending year; January-April retain their calendar year. Season 2024 therefore means winter 2023-24.\n\nThe October-April window includes start timestamps from October 1 at 04:00 through April 30 at 16:00; its last observation period ends May 1 at 04:00. Monthly assignment likewise uses the period-start date, not the ending date. No daylight-saving conversion or boundary-period split is applied.\n\nBuild the expected grid at 12-hour spacing. Non-leap winters have 424 periods and leap winters 426. Reject duplicate timestamps, out-of-window records, nonfinite or negative snow values. Monthly completeness compares unique records with twice the month's actual day count. Missing periods are NOT snowfall zeroes.\n\nSeven historical winters have missing October periods:\n\n| Winter | Missing October days | Missing periods |\n|---|---|---:|\n| 1999-2000 | 1-31 | 62 |\n| 2000-01 | 1-8 and 12 | 18 |\n| 2001-02 | 1-2 | 4 |\n| 2003-04 | 1-12 | 24 |\n| 2005-06 | 3-10 | 16 |\n| 2019-20 | 1-31 | 62 |\n| 2020-21 | 1-31 | 62 |\n\nAll November-April periods are present in every winter; the target October is also complete. Exactly 17 of the 24 historical winters have complete October-April coverage.\n\n## Seasonal analysis\n\nSum the original 12-hour snow values within each window. Compute historical arithmetic mean, median and sample standard deviation (denominator n-1), excluding the target winter. Difference = target minus historical mean; percent difference = 100 times difference divided by mean. Rank including the target = 1 plus the number of historical values strictly greater than the target. Ties share the same descending competition rank. `fraction_lower` counts historical values strictly below the target, divided by n; it is an empirical fraction, not a fitted return-period probability.\n\nThe primary October-April baseline includes only the 17 complete earlier winters. The common-window baseline uses November-April for all 24 earlier winters. `available_oct_apr` is a diagnostic pooled comparison that includes incomplete sums; it is NOT the preferred scientific baseline.\n\n## Monthly analysis\n\n1. Aggregate observations into 25 winters x 7 months = 175 rows.\n2. For incomplete October months, leave `snowfall_in` blank/null; retain the partial sum in `available_sum_in` and record `missing_periods`.\n3. Compare each 2023-24 month against earlier observations of that month under two cohorts:\n   - `matched17`: the SAME 17 complete-season historical winters for every month. Monthly means add to the primary seasonal mean.\n   - `available`: all complete historical months, n=17 for October and n=24 for November-April. This maximizes monthly coverage but does not define a matched-cohort seven-month seasonal mean.\n4. Compute mean, median, observed min/max and quartiles using `statistics.quantiles(n=4, method='inclusive')` (linear interpolation with positions (n-1)p).\n5. Compute anomalies, percent anomalies and descending ranks as above. Historical sample sizes exclude the target; `rank_of` includes it.\n\nFebruary totals use 28 or 29 actual days; they are NOT normalized to a daily rate. This is appropriate for reproducing monthly accumulated snowfall, but not a day-normalized intensity comparison. Spread blocks describe historical variability, not confidence intervals.\n\n## Output fields and precision\n\n- Seasonal CSV: `end_year`, `winter`, available `snowfall_in`, `rows`, `expected`, `missing`, `duplicates`, `extra`, `minimum`, complete common-window `nov_apr_in`, available October sum `oct_in`, and chart `coverage`/`label` fields. In particular, `oct_in=0` where October is absent is only an available-data sum, not an imputation.\n- Monthly totals CSV: `winter`, `end_year`, `month`, `month_order` (1=Oct to 7=Apr), complete `snowfall_in` or null, `available_sum_in`, `missing_periods`, `complete_month`.\n- Monthly comparison CSV: `cohort`, `month`, `month_order`, `target_in`, `mean_in`, `median_in`, `q25_in`, `q75_in`, `min_in`, `max_in`, `anomaly_in`, `anomaly_pct`, `n_history`, `rank`, `rank_of`, `direction`.\n\nAll snow quantities with `_in` are inches. Seasonal and monthly totals round to 2 decimal places. Monthly means/anomalies round to 4 decimals and percentage anomalies to 2. Percentages are calculated before rounding. Monthly contributions sum the stored 4-decimal anomalies, reproducing the conversation's rounding. Seasonal summary JSON retains Python floating-point precision.\n\n## Measurement and interpretation limits\n\nThe historical README describes quality control and conversion of trace snow to zero. The 2023-24 README describes raw Ski Patrol observations without additional QA. The monthly and seasonal audits validate timestamps and data shape, not instrument accuracy. Coverage excludes May-September, other Alta stations and winters outside these collections. Neither all-time rankings, trend attribution, causal weather explanations nor climatological return periods are estimated.\n\nSource licensing and recommended citations are in the original READMEs and `PROVENANCE.json`.\n","kind":"report","name":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-shareable-20261019\\alta-snowfall-17\\alta_reproducibility\\METHODS.md"}],"content":"","kind":"","name":"","path":"","pdf_preview":null,"purpose":"intermediate","used":null},"output":{"artifacts":[{"accepted":true,"artifact_id":"artifact_bd0c56f3e4a342e28b24c66ef1906fc8","created":true,"custody":"cas","kind":"report","mechanism":"model","name":"README.md","reason":"","sha256":"20c9a592ee294acb0908ff5374edb45a9e8e250113cb1ad908004d9b9e31e488","version":1},{"accepted":true,"artifact_id":"artifact_a92d00a729284c4e9527113d762626d3","created":true,"custody":"cas","kind":"report","mechanism":"model","name":"METHODS.md","reason":"","sha256":"1aa416fa6e6c9ca6147a6bd6edbb0a0a5430188ee2363f65f260d9789ef669e4","version":1}],"created":2,"deduplicated":0,"pdf_previews":[],"promotion_budget":{"limit":8,"remaining":6,"used":2},"rejected":0},"presentation":{"summary":"2 artifacts: 2 created, 0 deduplicated, 0 rejected (README.md, METHODS.md)","blocks":[{"id":"artifact-0","type":"link","media_type":"","text":"","label":"README.md","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"target":"artifact","uri":"artifact_bd0c56f3e4a342e28b24c66ef1906fc8","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""},{"id":"artifact-1","type":"link","media_type":"","text":"","label":"METHODS.md","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"target":"artifact","uri":"artifact_a92d0